17592186383055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166864 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166864 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166864 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166975 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166975 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166975 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166975 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166975 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166975 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166975 #edb/ref 1045 "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166975 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166975 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166975 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "adc0eea0-4c11-5878-9c7e-48c4aba23d56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167691 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167691 #edb/ref 1061 32405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167691 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1992388a-9592-55ad-8541-266b5f3c9669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167691 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167698 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167698 #edb/ref 1061 32593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167698 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "774cb7e3-782d-56db-8b29-9c8786c0396b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167698 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167747 #edb/ref 1061 35579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167747 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "218a6bf7-b94b-5fa6-bb10-cfd37d79dc0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167747 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167748 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167748 #edb/ref 1061 35582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167748 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a21469f-f98e-54de-806c-b0901c562c75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167748 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167749 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167749 #edb/ref 1061 35586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167749 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d7a42f9a-6688-59d2-b357-eb5701925961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167749 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168082 #edb/ref 1064 "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is **conservative** if there exists a scalar function $f$ such that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla \\! f.\n$$\n\nIf such a function $f$ exists, it is called a **potential function** for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nFor example, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$ is a potential function for $\\mathbf F$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\  & =F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNot all vector fields are conservative. However, conservative vector fields have many interesting and useful properties, as we'll soon discover." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168082 #edb/ref 1065 5152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168082 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b1462096-7305-5fe4-ae99-253541521cba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168082 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168124 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that this is equivalent to saying that the scalar curl is zero everywhere on $D,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0.$$\n\nIf $D\\neq\\mathbb R^2,$ then the situation gets a little trickier. We'll consider this case in a separate lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168124 #edb/ref 1065 5806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168124 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9a5b26c9-caa5-579e-a57c-c42b07eebafe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168124 #edb/ref 1067 "A Test to Determine Whether a Given Vector Field is Conservative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238821 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238821 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238821 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "58f11833-470e-5bef-bed8-6e5dd7f35fe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239046 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239046 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239046 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "73bfaf49-d235-5587-b751-402d142ac763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239516 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239516 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239516 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a836c130-129a-569d-9e28-b1933339a9c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239589 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239589 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239589 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b09885ad-d9c7-552d-b4c7-930d5c59c1e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240276 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240276 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240276 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fb3240e0-b339-53b6-a701-9e9f4809ca9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240472 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186240276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240472 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240472 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240472 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186239589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240472 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4fa88a49-b814-5ee5-80f8-03e880fa2400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240898 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240898 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240898 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=(2x-y)\\,i-x\\,j$\n- $G=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j$\n- $H={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240898 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ff94cca-f0c0-5b4f-9e96-5ccdc57e1837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240898 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-y$ and $Q=-x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={e}^{x-y}$ and $Q=-{e}^{x-y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{x-y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{e}^{x-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410562 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186410577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410562 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186410578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410562 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186410579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410562 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410562 #edb/ref 1045 "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410562 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186410579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410562 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410562 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410562 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5445d5ff-8221-5ab1-9733-fcfbdf6c3d6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410563 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410563 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410563 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3575b062-a274-5475-804d-55979057dd00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410564 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410564 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #ed